# Settings of the Compose stack. Do not copy this file by hand:
#
#   deploy/init-env.sh
#
# creates .env from it and makes every secret below, each a fresh random
# value. The stack refuses to start with the placeholders, which are public.
#
# On a new install the secrets are not written here but into files, one for
# each, in the directory NIK_SECRETS_DIR names (./secrets): each container is
# given the files it uses and no other, and a secret is in the environment of
# none. Their lines stay below, empty. A stack from before 6.8 keeps them here
# until deploy/secrets.sh move is run. Never fill a secret in both places:
# the stack refuses it.
#
# Keep a copy of the secrets away from the server: SECRET_KEY with your
# backups, STORE_KEY apart from them.
# NIK_SECRETS_DIR=./secrets

# PostgreSQL password (used by both the db and api services). Any character
# is fine; only if it contains # or $ wrap the value in single quotes. It is
# set when the database is first created: to change it later, change it in
# PostgreSQL too (ALTER ROLE nik PASSWORD '…').
DB_PASSWORD=change-this-db-password

# Encrypts the secrets stored in the database (keys and credentials of
# connector instances, second factors, the backup passphrase, TLS key logs of
# captures). Without it they cannot be read again: keep it with the backups.
# To change it: deploy/secrets.sh rotate secret-key (Settings > Keys), which
# keeps the earlier key in SECRET_KEY_PREVIOUS while the change is under way.
SECRET_KEY=change-this-to-a-long-random-string
#SECRET_KEY_PREVIOUS=

# Seals every file of the store (samples, e-mails, captures, mailboxes): on
# the disk nothing is readable without it. It is in no database and in no
# backup (a backup holds the files under its own passphrase): if it is lost
# with the server, the files come back from a backup, under the new key of
# the server that restores. Keep a copy away from the server all the same.
# To change it: deploy/secrets.sh rotate store-key (Settings > Keys), which
# keeps the earlier key in STORE_KEY_PREVIOUS while the change is under way.
STORE_KEY=change-this-store-key
#STORE_KEY_PREVIOUS=
# How fast the store of a server from before 6.8 is sealed, in megabytes a
# second (0: as fast as the disk goes). Settings > Storage shows how far it is.
# STORE_SEAL_MBPS=40

# Signs every entry of the audit log: whoever can write to the database
# cannot make the signatures again. A new install gets one of its own from
# deploy/init-env.sh. Left empty (a server from before 6.8), the entries are
# signed with a key derived from SECRET_KEY, until you choose to give the
# log a key of its own: deploy/secrets.sh rotate audit-key (Settings > Keys).
# The earlier keys stay in AUDIT_KEY_PREVIOUS (several, separated by commas):
# the entries they signed are checked with them. Keep a copy of both away
# from the server, where the copy of SECRET_KEY is.
AUDIT_KEY=
#AUDIT_KEY_PREVIOUS=

# Host port for plain HTTP. It serves nothing: every request is redirected to
# the HTTPS port below.
WEB_PORT=8080

# Host port for HTTPS. A self-signed certificate is generated on first start
# unless you mount your own into the web-certs volume (see the README).
WEB_TLS_PORT=8443

# Hostname embedded in the auto-generated certificate (CN + SAN). Set it to
# whatever users type in the browser, e.g. nik.example.internal.
TLS_HOSTNAME=localhost

# Session lifetime (hours).
SESSION_TTL_HOURS=12

# The app is served over HTTPS only, so the session cookie is Secure. Leave it.
# COOKIE_SECURE=true

# Who the backend believes about the client's address: the web container.
# Change it only if something else than this stack's nginx hands requests to
# the backend (host names, addresses or networks, separated by commas).
# TRUSTED_PROXIES=web

# Limits of the backend container (it parses the evidence).
API_MEMORY=4g
API_CPUS=4

# Memory limit of the web container (nginx).
# WEB_MEMORY=512m

# Optional: a token Prometheus must send (Authorization: Bearer …) to read
# /api/metrics, accepted beside the scrape token made on the health page. The
# endpoint is reached through the web port like the rest of /api and answers
# to a token only, never to a session.
#METRICS_TOKEN=

# Per-file size limit for malware samples uploaded to the Malware Analysis tab.
SAMPLE_MAX_MB=100

# Per-file size limit for the evidence files the server parses (browser
# databases, event logs, shell histories, tool output) through the API.
ARTIFACT_MAX_MB=500

# Analyzer worker (rizin) for the Code view: shared secret between the api and
# analyzer services (use a long random value, e.g. `openssl rand -hex 32`),
# per-sample time limit in seconds, the part of it spent decompiling functions
# up front (the rest are decompiled when first opened), the safety cap on the
# functions listed per sample, the analysed sessions kept open so that
# decompiling on demand takes seconds (each holds ~1 GB on a large binary; 0
# disables) and how long an unused one stays open, and the worker's memory /
# CPU limits.
ANALYZER_TOKEN=please-change-this-analyzer-token
ANALYZER_TIMEOUT=600
ANALYZER_MAX_JOBS=2
ANALYZER_DECOMPILE_SECONDS=300
ANALYZER_MAX_FUNCTIONS=20000
ANALYZER_WARM_SESSIONS=1
ANALYZER_WARM_IDLE=600
ANALYZER_MEMORY=4g
ANALYZER_CPUS=2

# capa worker for the Capabilities view: its own shared secret, per-sample
# time limit in seconds (large binaries take ten minutes or more) and the
# worker's memory / CPU limits.
CAPA_TOKEN=please-change-this-capa-token
CAPA_TIMEOUT=1800
CAPA_MEMORY=4g
CAPA_CPUS=2

# Mail attachment previews: document viewer (LibreOffice + pdfium) and
# isolated browser (Chromium). Their own shared secrets and limits.
DOCVIEW_TOKEN=please-change-this-docview-token
DOCVIEW_MEMORY=2g
DOCVIEW_CPUS=1
# Largest Outlook PST accepted (MB). It is converted on the worker's pst-work volume, which needs
# about 2.5 times the PST free while it is converted.
# PST_MAX_MB=4096
BROWSER_TOKEN=please-change-this-browser-token
BROWSER_MAX_SESSIONS=3
BROWSER_MEMORY=2g
BROWSER_CPUS=2

# Packet capture worker (Network Analysis, Wireshark's engine): its shared
# secret, the largest capture accepted (MB) and its memory / CPU limits
# (sharkd keeps an open capture in memory: a few times its size). The worker
# is sent the capture it reads: one of PCAP_MEMORY_FILE_MB or less is kept in
# memory, a larger one on its work volume, in clear, while it is open.
PCAP_TOKEN=please-change-this-pcap-token
PCAP_MAX_MB=500
# PCAP_MEMORY_FILE_MB=64
PCAP_MEMORY=4g
PCAP_CPUS=2

# Script sandbox (the Python scripts of playbooks): its shared secret, how long
# a script may run by default and at most (seconds), the memory of one script
# (MB) and the limits of the container.
SANDBOX_TOKEN=please-change-this-sandbox-token
# SANDBOX_TIMEOUT=10
# SANDBOX_MAX_TIMEOUT=60
# SANDBOX_MEMORY_MB=256
# SANDBOX_MEMORY=512m
# SANDBOX_CPUS=1

# Content packs. The catalogue of packs is read from the release "packs" of
# Nik's repository unless another address is given here or set in Settings;
# an empty value (NIK_PACKS_CATALOGUE=) means no catalogue: packs come as
# files. NIK_PACKS_DIR is a folder inside the backend container (mount it with
# a Compose override) whose packs are installed at start.
# NIK_PACKS_CATALOGUE=https://github.com/n36l3c7/Nik/releases/download/packs/index.json
# NIK_PACKS_DIR=

# Backups. The server takes them by itself (Settings > System > Backup) into the
# volume "backups" of the stack; NIK_BACKUP_DIR names a directory of the host
# instead: another disk than the data. Before a release migrates the database
# it takes a snapshot there and tries the migrations on a copy; false skips
# that rehearsal (never the snapshot) on a database too large to copy each time.
# NIK_BACKUP_DIR=/srv/nik-backups
# NIK_UPGRADE_REHEARSAL=true

# Recovery to a moment, and a standby host (Settings > System > Backup, at the
# foot). PostgreSQL archives its log into the volume "wal-archive" and the
# recovery service takes a base backup every day: with the two the database
# can be brought back to any moment (deploy/restore.sh --to). NIK_WAL_ARCHIVE=off
# keeps no archive. A segment of the log is closed at least every
# NIK_ARCHIVE_TIMEOUT seconds while something is written: the most that is
# lost when the host dies; lower loses less and writes more small files.
# NIK_WAL_ARCHIVE_DIR names a directory of the host instead of the volume:
# another disk than the data (what it holds is in clear, like the database).
# NIK_WAL_ARCHIVE=on
# NIK_ARCHIVE_TIMEOUT=300
# NIK_WAL_ARCHIVE_DIR=/srv/nik-archive
# On a standby: the directory of this host the first host sends its copies to.
# deploy/standby.sh init writes it; leave it alone on any other server.
# NIK_STANDBY_INBOX=
# What this server calls itself to the other host of a pair (default:
# TLS_HOSTNAME), and the limits of the recovery container.
# NIK_SERVER_NAME=
# RECOVERY_MEMORY=1g
# RECOVERY_CPUS=2

# Automation (playbooks). Where the loop that makes them run lives: container
# (the automation service, the default), inline (a thread of the backend) or
# off. How many steps run side by side, how many runs may be in progress on
# the server, how many finished runs each playbook keeps; a proxy for what
# playbooks send outside (empty when the server has a direct or transparent
# way out); the limits of the automation container.
# AUTOMATION_WORKER=container
# AUTOMATION_CONCURRENCY=4
# AUTOMATION_MAX_ACTIVE_RUNS=200
# AUTOMATION_RUNS_KEPT=500
# AUTOMATION_HTTPS_PROXY=
# AUTOMATION_NO_PROXY=
# AUTOMATION_MEMORY=1g
# AUTOMATION_CPUS=2
